# The guards that keep this repo honest, and the things they guard. `.github/`
# is self-owning, so neither this file nor the CI wiring can change without an
# owner's review. GitHub applies the LAST matching pattern for a given path —
# every pattern here names the same owner and none of them overlap, so order
# is not load-bearing today, but a later pattern that overlaps an earlier one
# and names a different owner would win.
/.github/            @chav-aniket
/scripts/            @chav-aniket
/bunfig.toml         @chav-aniket

# The indirection the guards run through. Owning the workflow without owning
# these leaves the cheapest bypass unreviewed: every CI step calls a guard by
# script name out of `package.json`, `oxlintrc.json` decides that the vendored
# oxlint plugin is the one that runs, and `lefthook.yml` is the local half.
/package.json        @chav-aniket
/oxlintrc.json       @chav-aniket
/lefthook.yml        @chav-aniket

# Vendored upstream source (see tools/oxlint/anti-slop/README.md). An edit
# here bypasses oxfmt and oxlint by design — only the SHA256SUMS check in CI
# catches drift — so a change to this tree gets a human owner's eyes.
/tools/oxlint/**     @chav-aniket
